{"id": "effect-f3308fae-cb5d-54b6-a8bc-30b9b42cb77d", "sessionId": "0d98d73f-4d2c-9de8-9f17-676d8474ade1", "turnId": "turn-97a6c9f894655e8f6c018258f926a4d7", "createdAt": 1792352480449, "updatedAt": 1792352480500, "generationStatus": "completed", "relatedEntryId": null, "type": "effect", "title": "file_system.read_file", "detail": {"toolName": "file_system.read_file", "display": {"summary": "Reading C:/Github/ANYsolver/src/anysolver/corotational.py", "content": null, "suffix": "", "verb": "Reading", "message": "C:/Github/ANYsolver/src/anysolver/corotational.py", "settledVerb": "Read", "settledMessage": "C:/Github/ANYsolver/src/anysolver/corotational.py", "statusText": "Reading file"}, "kind": "file_read", "input": {"filePath": "C:/Github/ANYsolver/src/anysolver/corotational.py", "offset": 1191, "limit": 130}}, "state": {"status": "completed", "output": {"filePath": "C:\\Github\\ANYsolver\\src\\anysolver\\corotational.py", "content": "        c2 = float((1.0 - np.cos(alpha)) / (alpha * alpha))\r\n    return c1 * K + c2 * (K @ K)\r\n\r\n\r\nclass SO3MeanDomainError(ValueError):\r\n    \"\"\"An intrinsic SO(3) mean input left its supported convex/chart domain.\r\n\r\n    Raised by the domain guards of :func:`_so3_weighted_mean` (principal\r\n    rotation-vector chart, unique two-master geodesic, convex anchor ball,\r\n    mean chart boundary, singular tangent operator).  It is a ``ValueError``\r\n    for backward compatibility, but carries a distinct type so callers can\r\n    classify a *state-domain* refusal of a trial point (for example a\r\n    line-search trial whose master rotations left the chart) instead of\r\n    aborting on an arbitrary exception.  Input-contract violations (finite\r\n    rotations/weights, nonnegative partition weights) remain plain\r\n    ``ValueError``.\r\n    \"\"\"\r\n\r\n\r\ndef _so3_weighted_mean(\r\n    master_thetas,\r\n    weights,\r\n    label,\r\n):\r\n    \"\"\"Intrinsic weighted SO(3) mean with exact tangent derivatives.\r\n\r\n    Returns ``(theta_m, (G_i, ...))`` where ``R(theta_m)`` solves the\r\n    Karcher-type condition ``sum_i w_i Log(R_m^T R_i) = 0`` and\r\n    ``G_i = dtheta_m/dtheta_i`` is its exact differential in additive\r\n    rotation-vector coordinates:\r\n\r\n        Ghat_i = R_m B^{-1} w_i J_l(psi_i)^{-1} R_m^T      (spatial),\r\n        G_i    = J_l(theta_m)^{-1} Ghat_i J_l(theta_i),\r\n        B      = sum_i w_i J_l(psi_i)^{-1},  psi_i = Log(R_m^T R_i).\r\n\r\n    The spatial blocks satisfy ``sum_i Ghat_i = I``, which makes the mean\r\n    left-equivariant: for a common superposed rotation ``Q``,\r\n    ``mean(Q R_i, w) = Q mean(R_i, w)``, and the parameter tangents satisfy\r\n    the differential rigid-spin identity\r\n    ``sum_i G_i J_l(theta_i)^{-1} = J_l(theta_m)^{-1}``, the covector\r\n    counterpart of physical wrench preservation.\r\n\r\n    Two-master means use the unique shortest-geodesic closed form.  Larger\r\n    means are restricted to an open pi/4 ball about the first master, with\r\n    the returned mean inside that same ball.  Multi-master input/output\r\n    rotation vectors must remain inside the supported principal chart.\r\n    Nonnegative partition weights are required; their common scale cancels\r\n    from the mean equation and its differential.  The direct route keeps\r\n    its existing rotation coordinates.\r\n    \"\"\"\r\n    thetas = [np.asarray(t, dtype=float).reshape(3).copy() for t in master_thetas]\r\n    ws = [float(w) for w in weights]\r\n    if not thetas or len(thetas) != len(ws):\r\n        raise ValueError(f\"{label}: SO(3) mean requires at least one master\")\r\n    if not all(np.all(np.isfinite(t)) for t in thetas) or not np.all(np.isfinite(ws)):\r\n        raise ValueError(f\"{label}: SO(3) mean requires finite rotations and weights\")\r\n    if any(w < 0.0 for w in ws):\r\n        raise ValueError(\r\n            f\"{label}: the intrinsic SO(3) mean on the active rotating-offset \"\r\n            f\"route requires nonnegative weights; got {ws}\"\r\n        )\r\n    weight_sum = float(sum(ws))\r\n    if abs(weight_sum - 1.0) > 1.0e-8:\r\n        raise ValueError(\r\n            f\"{label}: the intrinsic SO(3) mean requires weights forming a \"\r\n            f\"partition of unity; got sum {weight_sum!r}\"\r\n        )\r\n    if len(thetas) == 1:\r\n        return thetas[0], (np.eye(3),)\r\n    ws = [w / weight_sum for w in ws]\r\n    chart_limit = np.pi - 1.0e-6\r\n    if any(float(np.linalg.norm(t)) >= chart_limit for t in thetas):\r\n        raise SO3MeanDomainError(f\"{label}: interpolated SO(3) mean is outside the principal rotation-vector chart\")\r\n    first = thetas[0]\r\n    if all(np.array_equal(t, first) for t in thetas[1:]):\r\n        return first.copy(), tuple(w * np.eye(3) for w in ws)\r\n\r\n    rotations = [rotation_matrix_from_vector(t) for t in thetas]\r\n    anchor = rotations[0]\r\n    if len(thetas) == 2:\r\n        relative = rotation_vector_from_matrix(anchor.T @ rotations[1])\r\n        if float(np.linalg.norm(relative)) >= chart_limit:\r\n            raise SO3MeanDomainError(f\"{label}: ambiguous shortest-geodesic SO(3) mean\")\r\n        fraction = ws[1]\r\n        R_m = anchor @ rotation_matrix_from_vector(fraction * relative)\r\n        psis = [-fraction * relative, (1.0 - fraction) * relative]\r\n    else:\r\n        radius = np.pi / 4.0\r\n        if any(float(np.linalg.norm(rotation_vector_from_matrix(anchor.T @ r))) >= radius\r\n               for r in rotations):\r\n            raise SO3MeanDomainError(f\"{label}: SO(3) mean requires a unique convex anchor ball of radius pi/4\")\r\n        R_m = anchor.copy()\r\n        for _ in range(100):\r\n            psis = [rotation_vector_from_matrix(R_m.T @ r) for r in rotations]\r\n            f = sum((w * psi for w, psi in zip(ws, psis)), np.zeros(3))\r\n            if float(np.linalg.norm(f)) <= 8.0 * np.finfo(float).eps:\r\n                break\r\n            R_m = R_m @ rotation_matrix_from_vector(f)\r\n        else:\r\n            raise SO3MeanDomainError(f\"{label}: SO(3) mean failed to converge in the convex anchor ball\")\r\n        if float(np.linalg.norm(rotation_vector_from_matrix(anchor.T @ R_m))) >= radius:\r\n            raise SO3MeanDomainError(f\"{label}: SO(3) mean left the unique convex anchor ball\")\r\n    theta_m = rotation_vector_from_matrix(R_m)\r\n    if float(np.linalg.norm(theta_m)) >= chart_limit:\r\n        raise SO3MeanDomainError(f\"{label}: SO(3) mean reached the principal rotation-vector chart boundary\")\r\n    A = [_rotation_left_jacobian_inverse(psi) for psi in psis]\r\n    B = np.zeros((3, 3), dtype=float)\r\n    for a, w in zip(A, ws):\r\n        B += w * a\r\n    if float(np.min(np.linalg.eigvalsh(0.5 * (B + B.T)))) <= 0.0:\r\n        raise SO3MeanDomainError(\r\n            f\"{label}: intrinsic SO(3) mean tangent operator is singular; \"\r\n            \"ambiguous mean refused\"\r\n        )\r\n    B_inv = np.linalg.inv(B)\r\n    Jl_m_inv = _rotation_left_jacobian_inverse(theta_m)\r\n    G = []\r\n    for t, a, w in zip(thetas, A, ws):\r\n        Ghat = R_m @ (B_inv @ (w * a)) @ R_m.T\r\n        Jl_i = _rotation_right_jacobian(t).T\r\n        G.append(Jl_m_inv @ Ghat @ Jl_i)\r\n    return theta_m, tuple(G)\r\n\r\n\r\ndef rotating_mpc_lever_arm_jacobian(\r\n    theta_bar: np.ndarray,\r\n    eccentricity: np.ndarray,\r\n) -> np.ndarray:\r\n    \"\"\"Return ``A(theta_bar, r_0) = d((R(theta_bar) - I) r_0) / d(theta_bar)``.\r\n\r\n", "numLines": 130, "startLine": 1191, "requestedOffset": 1191, "requestedLimit": 130, "totalLines": null, "wasTruncated": true}, "outputText": "", "durationMs": 0.0, "display": {"success": true, "verb": "Read", "message": "130 lines from C:\\Github\\ANYsolver\\src\\anysolver\\corotational.py", "warnings": [], "approvalNote": null, "suffix": "(truncated)"}, "decision": "execute", "approvalType": "always", "approvalSource": "bypass"}}
{"id": "effect-d584064f-2511-5110-9c7d-0a2bda5e22c5", "sessionId": "0d98d73f-4d2c-9de8-9f17-676d8474ade1", "turnId": "turn-97a6c9f894655e8f6c018258f926a4d7", "createdAt": 1792352480458, "updatedAt": 1792352480515, "generationStatus": "completed", "relatedEntryId": null, "type": "effect", "title": "file_system.read_file", "detail": {"toolName": "file_system.read_file", "display": {"summary": "Reading C:/Github/ANYsolver/src/anysolver/corotational.py", "content": null, "suffix": "", "verb": "Reading", "message": "C:/Github/ANYsolver/src/anysolver/corotational.py", "settledVerb": "Read", "settledMessage": "C:/Github/ANYsolver/src/anysolver/corotational.py", "statusText": "Reading file"}, "kind": "file_read", "input": {"filePath": "C:/Github/ANYsolver/src/anysolver/corotational.py", "offset": 1541, "limit": 180}}, "state": {"status": "completed", "output": {"filePath": "C:\\Github\\ANYsolver\\src\\anysolver\\corotational.py", "content": "    def reduce_force(self, u_current: np.ndarray, full_force: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\r\n        \"\"\"Return ``J(u)^T full_force`` without materializing ``J(u)``.\"\"\"\r\n        f = np.asarray(full_force, dtype=float).reshape(-1)\r\n        if not self.entries:\r\n            return np.asarray(self.T_linear.T @ f, dtype=float).reshape(-1)\r\n        u = np.asarray(u_current, dtype=float).reshape(-1)\r\n        f_mod = f.copy()\r\n        for entry in self.entries:\r\n            f_b = f[entry.slave_u_dofs]\r\n            m_b = f[entry.slave_theta_dofs]\r\n            if float(np.linalg.norm(f_b)) <= 1.0e-30 and float(np.linalg.norm(m_b)) <= 1.0e-30:\r\n                continue\r\n            theta_m, G_list = entry.mean_state(u)\r\n            A = rotating_mpc_lever_arm_jacobian(theta_m, entry.eccentricity)\r\n            v = A.T @ f_b + m_b\r\n            const = entry.r_skew.T @ f_b - m_b\r\n            for (_m_u, m_theta, w), G in zip(entry.masters, G_list):\r\n                f_mod[m_theta] += G.T @ v + w * const\r\n        return np.asarray(self.T_linear.T @ f_mod, dtype=float).reshape(-1)\r\n\r\n    def geometric_tangent(\r\n        self,\r\n        u_current: np.ndarray,\r\n        full_imbalance: np.ndarray,\r\n    ) -> Any:\r\n        \"\"\"Return ``K_mpc,geom(u, F_int - F_ext) = d(J^T F)/dq`` (F frozen).\r\n\r\n        The complete second derivative of the slave correction is included:\r\n        second derivatives of the intrinsic mean and of the slave\r\n        orientation contracted with the full slave rotation imbalance\r\n        ``m_b``, and offset-translation derivatives contracted with ``f_b``.\r\n        It is evaluated by central finite differences of the exact\r\n        per-master covector correction of :meth:`reduce_force`, so it stays\r\n        consistent with the forward map by construction.\r\n        \"\"\"\r\n        from scipy import sparse\r\n\r\n        if not self.entries or self.n_red == 0:\r\n            return sparse.csr_matrix((self.n_red, self.n_red), dtype=float)\r\n        u = np.asarray(u_current, dtype=float).reshape(-1)\r\n        imbalance = np.asarray(full_imbalance, dtype=float).reshape(-1)\r\n        terms = []\r\n        h = 1.0e-6\r\n        for entry in self.entries:\r\n            projs = entry.master_theta_projs\r\n            if all(p.nnz == 0 for p in projs):\r\n                continue\r\n            f_b = imbalance[entry.slave_u_dofs]\r\n            m_b = imbalance[entry.slave_theta_dofs]\r\n            if float(np.linalg.norm(f_b)) <= 1.0e-30 and float(np.linalg.norm(m_b)) <= 1.0e-30:\r\n                continue\r\n            thetas = entry.master_rotations(u)\r\n            weights = [w for _m_u, _m_theta, w in entry.masters]\r\n\r\n            def covectors(state_thetas):\r\n                theta_m, G_list = _so3_weighted_mean(\r\n                    state_thetas, weights, entry.label_prefix\r\n                )\r\n                A = rotating_mpc_lever_arm_jacobian(theta_m, entry.eccentricity)\r\n                vv = A.T @ f_b + m_b\r\n                return [G.T @ vv for G in G_list]\r\n\r\n            n_m = len(projs)\r\n            D = [[np.zeros((3, 3), dtype=float) for _ in range(n_m)] for _ in range(n_m)]\r\n            for j in range(n_m):\r\n                for c in range(3):\r\n                    plus = [t.copy() for t in thetas]\r\n                    minus = [t.copy() for t in thetas]\r\n                    plus[j][c] += h\r\n                    minus[j][c] -= h\r\n                    cp = covectors(plus)\r\n                    cm = covectors(minus)\r\n                    for i in range(n_m):\r\n                        D[i][j][:, c] = (cp[i] - cm[i]) / (2.0 * h)\r\n            for i in range(n_m):\r\n                for j in range(n_m):\r\n                    if not np.any(D[i][j]):\r\n                        continue\r\n                    term = projs[i].T @ sparse.csr_matrix(D[i][j]) @ projs[j]\r\n                    if term.nnz:\r\n                        terms.append(term)\r\n        if not terms:\r\n            return sparse.csr_matrix((self.n_red, self.n_red), dtype=float)\r\n        total = terms[0]\r\n        for term in terms[1:]:\r\n            total = total + term\r\n        total = (total + total.T).tocsr()\r\n        return 0.5 * total\r\n\r\n    def reduce_tangent(\r\n        self,\r\n        u_current: np.ndarray,\r\n        K_T: Any,\r\n        full_imbalance: Optional[np.ndarray] = None,\r\n        K_ext: Optional[Any] = None,\r\n    ) -> Any:\r\n        \"\"\"Return ``J(u)^T (K_T - K_ext) J(u) + K_mpc,geom(u, full_imbalance)``.\"\"\"\r\n        J = self.jacobian(u_current)\r\n        K_red = (J.T @ K_T @ J).tocsr()\r\n        if K_ext is not None and getattr(K_ext, \"nnz\", 0):\r\n            K_red = (K_red - (J.T @ K_ext @ J)).tocsr()\r\n        if full_imbalance is not None:\r\n            K_geom = self.geometric_tangent(u_current, full_imbalance)\r\n            if K_geom.nnz:\r\n                K_red = (K_red + K_geom).tocsr()\r\n        return K_red\r\n\r\n    def transfer_slave_imbalance_to_masters(\r\n        self,\r\n        u_current: np.ndarray,\r\n        full_imbalance: np.ndarray,\r\n    ) -> np.ndarray:\r\n        \"\"\"Transfer slave forces/moments to master shell DOFs for support reactions.\r\n\r\n        The slave force ``f_b`` is distributed with the linear weights to the\r\n        master translations; the mean-chart slave moment covector\r\n        ``m_b + A(theta_m)^T f_b`` is distributed through the transposed mean\r\n        tangents ``G_i^T`` to the master rotation covectors.  This is the\r\n        exact adjoint of the nonlinear slave orientation map, so the total\r\n        physical spatial wrench is preserved (the old common-covector\r\n        shortcut left a moment defect ``D^T (m_b + A^T f_b)``).\r\n        \"\"\"\r\n        imbalance = np.asarray(full_imbalance, dtype=float).reshape(-1).copy()\r\n        if not self.entries:\r\n            return imbalance\r\n        u = np.asarray(u_current, dtype=float).reshape(-1)\r\n        for entry in self.entries:\r\n            f_b = imbalance[entry.slave_u_dofs].copy()\r\n            m_b = imbalance[entry.slave_theta_dofs].copy()\r\n            if float(np.linalg.norm(f_b)) <= 1.0e-30 and float(np.linalg.norm(m_b)) <= 1.0e-30:\r\n                imbalance[entry.slave_u_dofs] = 0.0\r\n                imbalance[entry.slave_theta_dofs] = 0.0\r\n                continue\r\n            theta_m, G_list = entry.mean_state(u)\r\n            A = rotating_mpc_lever_arm_jacobian(theta_m, entry.eccentricity)\r\n            moment_covector = m_b + A.T @ f_b\r\n            for (m_u, m_theta, w), G in zip(entry.masters, G_list):\r\n                imbalance[m_u] += w * f_b\r\n                imbalance[m_theta] += G.T @ moment_covector\r\n            imbalance[entry.slave_u_dofs] = 0.0\r\n            imbalance[entry.slave_theta_dofs] = 0.0\r\n        return imbalance\r\n\r\n\r\ndef has_nonlinear_rotating_mpc(model: \"FEModel\") -> bool:\r\n    \"\"\"Return True if ``model`` contains active rotating-arm beam-shell MPCs.\"\"\"\r\n    mesh = getattr(model, \"mesh\", None)\r\n    elements = getattr(mesh, \"elements\", None)\r\n    if not elements:\r\n        return False\r\n    for element in elements.values():\r\n        getter = getattr(element, \"get_nonlinear_mpc_descriptor\", None)\r\n        if callable(getter) and getter(mesh) is not None:\r\n            return True\r\n    return False\r\n\r\n\r\ndef _reject_downstream_rotating_slave_translation_dependencies(\r\n    model: \"FEModel\",\r\n    entries: Tuple[NonlinearRotatingMPCEntry, ...],\r\n) -> None:\r\n    \"\"\"Fail closed when an admitted affine equation consumes a rotating slave DOF.\r\n\r\n    The rotating map corrects each descriptor's own slave translations, and\r\n    the intrinsic SO(3) mean makes the slave rotation of every multi-master\r\n    coupling a nonlinear function of the master rotations.  Any other\r\n    admitted affine equation that uses one of those DOFs as a nonzero\r\n    master would silently retain the linearized value in the returned\r\n    physical displacement, so such compositions are refused before\r\n    constitutive activation instead of being solved on the wrong manifold.\r\n    The check uses the actual normalized audit equations, including their\r\n    coefficient filtering.  Ordinary linear chains without a rotating\r\n    descriptor and upstream shell row ties remain supported; single-master\r\n    direct slave rotations stay linear under the map and are not\r\n    restricted.  A currently identical multi-master state does not admit a\r\n    dependency that becomes nonlinear later.\r\n    \"\"\"\r\n    rotating_slave_translations: set = set()\r\n    nonlinear_slave_rotations: set = set()\r\n    for entry in entries:\r\n", "numLines": 180, "startLine": 1541, "requestedOffset": 1541, "requestedLimit": 180, "totalLines": null, "wasTruncated": true}, "outputText": "", "durationMs": 0.0, "display": {"success": true, "verb": "Read", "message": "180 lines from C:\\Github\\ANYsolver\\src\\anysolver\\corotational.py", "warnings": [], "approvalNote": null, "suffix": "(truncated)"}, "decision": "execute", "approvalType": "always", "approvalSource": "bypass"}}
{"id": "reasoning-587c494b-752a-5a4a-a8a3-468b5f29722d-0", "sessionId": "0d98d73f-4d2c-9de8-9f17-676d8474ade1", "turnId": "turn-97a6c9f894655e8f6c018258f926a4d7", "createdAt": 1792352493922, "updatedAt": 1792352493922, "generationStatus": "completed", "relatedEntryId": null, "type": "reasoning", "text": "Now I understand `geometric_tangent`. The counterexample: master thetas at norm pi-1.5e-6 pass the chart_limit guard (pi-1e-6), but the stencil `plus[j][c] += h` with h=1e-6 pushes norm to pi-1.5e-6+1e-6 = pi-0.5e-6 < pi but >= chart_limit (pi-1e-6), so `covectors(plus)` raises SO3MeanDomainError. Also the two-master geodesic guard and anchor-ball guard could be violated by stencil probes.\n\nDomain-safe differentiation: adapt the internal stencil so probes remain admitted. Options:\n1. Shrink h for a component when the probe would leave the domain. But detecting \"would leave the domain\" requires checking guards — we can pre-check: for each probe, check chart limit norm, two-master relative norm, anchor-ball radius. If a probe violates, reduce h (e.g., halve until admitted, down to some floor). If even tiny h fails (shouldn't, since center is admitted and domain is open), fall back to one-sided difference using the center (which is admitted).\n2. Use exact derivative — too complex.\n\nSimplest robust approach: for each (j, c) direction, compute a safe step h_jc:\n- Start with h = 1e-6.\n- Check both plus and minus probes against the domain predicates (chart limit on each theta norm; for two-master: relative rotation norm < chart_limit; for multi-master: anchor-ball radius). Actually the anchor-ball check involves rotations, but we can check via rotation vectors: norm of rotation_vector_from_matrix(anchor.T @ r) < pi/4. We can compute that for probes.\n- If a probe is inadmissible, halve h (say up to ~20 halvings) until both probes admitted. If h falls below a floor (e.g., 1e-12) and still inadmissible, fall back to one-sided difference from the admitted center: (covectors(plus) - covectors(center))/h with tiny h... but if plus is inadmissible for any h>0, the directional derivative may not exist in that chart. Hmm.\n\nWait — actually think about the counterexample: thetas = (pi-1.5e-6, 0, 0) for both masters. Center admitted (norm pi-1.5e-6 < pi-1e-6). Probe plus in +x direction: norm pi-0.5e-6 which is >= chart_limit → refused. Probe minus: pi-2.5e-6, admitted. So the derivative in +x direction at that point: the mean function isn't defined beyond chart_limit in that direction. But the derivative itself (one-sided) exists: the function is smooth up to the boundary. So a one-sided backward difference (covectors(center) - covectors(minus))/h is the right domain-safe estimate. Actually central difference with asymmetric step: use backward difference when plus is inadmissible, forward difference when minus is inadmissible.\n\nBut wait — accuracy: one-sided difference has O(h) error instead of O(h^2). With h=1e-6, error ~1e-6 * |f''| — could be acceptable? The task says \"adapt internal stencil to remain admitted, or compatible exact derivative\". Alternative: shrink h so both probes admitted — but here plus is inadmissible for ANY h>0 in that direction (norm already at pi-1.5e-6, any positive increment in that component increases norm beyond... wait no: plus[j][c] += h increases the component; norm becomes pi-1.5e-6+h. For h < 0.5e-6, norm < pi-1e-6 = chart_limit, so admitted! So shrinking h to below 0.5e-6 works. E.g., h_safe = min(h, (chart_limit - norm - eps)/|direction effect|).\n\nActually the guard is `norm >= chart_limit` raises. So we need norm(plus) < pi-1e-6. With center norm pi-1.5e-6, we need h < 0.5e-6. So halving h once (5e-7) suffices. Good — shrinking works for the chart-boundary case since the center is strictly inside the open domain.\n\nBut is the center always strictly inside? The center is admitted, meaning all guards pass with strict inequalities (guards use >= to raise). So there's a positive margin in every guard. By continuity, sufficiently small h keeps probes admitted. So the algorithm: for each direction, start with h=1e-6, while either probe inadmissible (checked by a lightweight predicate replicating the guards), halve h, up to say 40 times (h down to ~1e-18 — too small; floor at like 1e-12, ~20 halvings). If still inadmissible at floor, fall back to one-sided difference using the admitted center and the admitted probe (whichever side is admitted), or skip... Better: use one-sided with the admitted side.\n\nBut there's a subtlety: the predicate must replicate the exact guards of `_so3_weighted_mean`:\n1. All thetas finite (probes are finite).\n2. norm(theta) < chart_limit for each theta.\n3. If all thetas equal → returns early (fine).\n4. Two-master: relative = rotation_vector_from_matrix(anchor.T @ R_1); norm(relative) < chart_limit.\n5. Multi-master: for each r, norm(rotation_vector_from_matrix(anchor.T @ r)) < pi/4.\n6. After mean: theta_m norm < chart_limit, convergence, ball escape, singular B — these depend on the mean computation itself, not just inputs. The mean output guards could still fail even if input guards pass? For the two-master closed form: R_m = anchor @ R(fraction*relative); theta_m = rotation_vector_from_matrix(R_m). norm(theta_m) < chart_limit — since relative < chart_limit and fraction in [0,1], fraction*relative has norm <= norm(relative) < chart_limit... but rotation_vector_from_matrix(R_m) — R_m is anchor composed with small rotation; its rotation vector norm could exceed chart_limit even if each is inside? E.g., anchor at pi-1.5e-6 near boundary, relative small in a direction that adds up — norm could reach pi-1e-6? Possible in principle. And B singularity: B = w0*Jl_inv(psi0)+w1*Jl_inv(psi1); Jl_inv is positive definite for |psi|<pi, so B is PD for two masters with nonneg weights (not both zero). For multi-master, similar.\n\nRather than replicating all guards in a predicate, a cleaner approach: wrap `covectors` probe evaluation in