ll succeed as long as probes can be made admitted — which holds whenever the center is strictly inside all input guards and output guards are continuous... Output guards (mean chart boundary, ball escape, B singular): at the center they pass strictly (guards use >= with margins? mean chart boundary: norm(theta_m) >= chart_limit raises; admitted center has norm(theta_m) < chart_limit strictly). B singular: min eigenvalue > 0 strictly. By continuity of everything in thetas, small enough probes stay admitted. So halving always terminates in exact arithmetic; with floating point, h below ~1e-12 gives noisy differences but still finite. The difference quotient (cp - cm)/(2h) with h=1e-12: covector values O(1), difference O(1e-12), relative rounding error ~1e-16/1e-12 = 1e-4 — acceptable degradation in a rare boundary case. Fine.\n\nFallback if max halvings exhausted: use one-sided difference with the smallest admitted probe and the center covectors (center is admitted). If even that fails, raise SO3MeanDomainError — but then it leaks. Hmm. Actually let me think about whether one-sided fallback is even needed. The center is admitted with strict margins; halving 25 times reaches h=3e-14; the domain is open with the center strictly inside; probes at 3e-14 are inside unless the margin is smaller than 3e-14, which is essentially measure-zero. I'll implement: halve up to 30 times; if still failing, try one-sided (center + admitted side at final h); if both sides refused, re-raise the typed error (extremely unlikely; and the typed error is now handled by callers as classified refusal rather than abort — but at committed-state tangent assembly it would abort... the task says \"Typed domain exception must not just leak at next Newton iterate\" — meaning we shouldn't rely on the leak; the adaptive stencil is the fix. A re-raise in a truly degenerate case is honest failure).\n\nWait, also there's the \"relative geodesic/anchor boundaries\" mentioned: \"Relative geodesic/anchor boundaries analogous.\" The two-master relative guard and pi/4 anchor-ball guard are also inside `_so3_weighted_mean` and probed by the stencil; the try/except-halving approach covers all of them uniformly since it uses the actual guards. \n\nAlso note: `covectors` is called with `state_thetas` = probe thetas. But wait — there's another subtlety: `entry.mean_state(u)` vs the stencil using `entry.master_rotations(u)`. The stencil perturbs thetas directly. Fine.\n\nEfficiency: try/except per probe pair — no cost in the common case.\n\nNow, one more consideration: the task says \"adapt internal stencil to remain admitted, or compatible exact derivative\". Adaptive halving is the \"adapt internal stencil\" option. Good.\n\nAlso \"discriminating regression with nonzero rotational projections and slave imbalance\": a test where master rotations are near the chart boundary (e.g., both masters at (pi-1.5e-6, 0, 0)), with nonzero rotational projections (projs with nnz>0) and nonzero slave imbalance (f_b/m_b), such that the old fixed-h stencil raises SO3MeanDomainError but the new adaptive one returns a finite tangent. And check the tangent is accurate: compare against a reference computed with smaller safe h, or against one-sided analytic? We can verify the adaptive result matches a central difference with a manually-safe h (e.g., h=1e-8 which is admitted for this configuration: pi-1.5e-6+1e-8 < pi-1e-6 ✓). So the test: geometric_tangent at near-boundary state returns finite matrix close to reference central-difference with h=1e-8. Also test that fixed h=1e-6 probes would raise (reproduce the counterexample) — i.e., call _so3_weighted_mean with plus probe and assert SO3MeanDomainError, establishing discriminating power.\n\nNow the force-control path. Let me read nonlinear_static.py around lines 6961-7400 to see the force solve loop, and the DC handling at 4200-4360 for reference.", "summary": []}
