e test passes num_layers=5 to assembly; the element's declared layer count function is `_qualified_q4_layer_count`. Hmm, private but stable within repo tests. Tests using private helpers is acceptable in this repo? The test file imports `constraint_residual_summary`, `build_nonlinear_rotating_mpc_map` etc. — public-ish. Let me check if there's a public layer-count API. grep \"_qualified_q4_layer_count\" definition.\n\nFor beam: fiber count = len of y from `element._fiber_section_grid(config)` — needs FiberSectionPlasticityConfig. Private again.\n\nAlternative cleaner approach: \"model declared shapes\" could be interpreted as: the shapes the model/element itself declares when asked to produce a pristine state. E.g., `element.init_nonlinear_state(layers)` — used at e4_pl_element 3963: `super().init_nonlinear_state(layers)`. If ShellElement/QualifiedE4PLShellElement has `init_nonlinear_state(num_layers)` returning the declared initial state, then the test can create the pristine declared state and compare shapes field-by-field:\n\n```python\ndeclared = element.init_nonlinear_state(num_layers)\nfor field in (\"plastic_strain\", \"alpha\", \"layer_strain\"):\n    assert state[field].shape == declared[field].shape\n```\n\nThat's exactly \"model declared shapes\" — declared by the element's own initializer. And for beams, is there an init_nonlinear_state? Let me check BeamElement for init_nonlinear_state.", "summary": []}
